# Bare Workflow Setup (for the last 6 native features)

The app has been running on **Expo Managed Workflow** so far - everything up to
this point works with just `npx expo start` / `eas build`, no native code
needed. The remaining 6 features need real native Android code:

- Ad Blocker (system-wide, VPNService)
- Router Dashboard (already pure JS/fetch - does NOT need bare workflow)
- App Manager (installed apps list)
- WiFi Scanner (nearby networks)
- Fake App Detector (uses App Manager's native module)
- TV Remote (Android TV Remote Service protocol)

Correction from earlier estimate: **Router Dashboard does not need bare
workflow** - talking to a router's own web admin panel is just `fetch()`
calls, which works fine in managed workflow. Only App Manager, WiFi
Scanner, Fake App Detector, Ad Blocker, and TV Remote need native modules.

## Step 1: Run prebuild (one-time, local machine)

```bash
npx expo prebuild --platform android
```

This generates an `android/` folder with real native Android project files
(Kotlin/Java, Gradle). From this point on:
- You can still use most Expo JS APIs normally
- Native modules (added in upcoming batches) go inside `android/app/src/main/java/...`
- `android/` is gitignored (see `.gitignore`) - each developer/CI runs
  `expo prebuild` fresh rather than committing generated native code,
  UNLESS you start hand-editing native files, in which case remove
  `android/` from `.gitignore` and commit it.

## Step 2: Install a dev client to test native modules

Managed Expo Go app CANNOT run custom native modules. You need:

```bash
npx expo install expo-dev-client
eas build --profile development --platform android
```

Install the resulting APK on your test phone - this replaces Expo Go for
this project from now on.

## Step 3: Play Store caveat for QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES

The App Manager feature needs the `QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES` Android permission
to list all installed apps. **Google Play restricts this permission** -
apps are usually rejected unless the app's core purpose is something like
a launcher, antivirus, or file manager. Before publishing to Play Store:
- Read Google's current policy on QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES
- Be ready to justify it in the Play Console's permissions declaration form
- Alternative: request permission for specific known package names only
  (no QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES needed) if you only need to check for specific
  apps (useful for Fake App Detector's "known fake app" checks)

## Step 4: Upcoming native module batches

Each of the 5 remaining features will be delivered as:
1. A native Android module (Kotlin file(s) under `android/app/src/main/java/`)
2. A JS bridge file that calls the native module
3. The screen UI

Render deployment (the Telegram bot) is unaffected by any of this - the
bot is a separate plain Node.js project.
